\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Overview of the standard Kubernetes Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA) control architecture. The HPA controller periodically retrieves workload metrics through the Kubernetes metrics pipeline and computes the desired replica count using Equation~\ref {eq:hpa}. This control loop assumes that the selected metric accurately reflects workload intensity, an assumption that does not hold for persistent-connection workloads where active sessions remain largely independent of CPU utilization. The architecture is illustrated by the authors based on the Kubernetes HPA design~\cite  {nguyen2020hpa}.}}{4}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:hpa_arch}{{1}{4}{Overview of the standard Kubernetes Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA) control architecture. The HPA controller periodically retrieves workload metrics through the Kubernetes metrics pipeline and computes the desired replica count using Equation~\ref {eq:hpa}. This control loop assumes that the selected metric accurately reflects workload intensity, an assumption that does not hold for persistent-connection workloads where active sessions remain largely independent of CPU utilization. The architecture is illustrated by the authors based on the Kubernetes HPA design~\cite {nguyen2020hpa}}{figure.1}{}}
